::: {.callout-note}
## How to read this table
Read one row, one column at a time. Take the row "Log GDP per capita" in column (3):

- **Coefficient** (5.31): the difference in life expectancy, in years, associated with one more unit of log GDP per capita, holding the Gini fixed.
- **Standard error** (in parentheses, 0.34): the uncertainty on the coefficient. A rough rule: the coefficient is "distinguishable from zero" if it is more than about 2 standard errors away (here 15.6).
- **Stars**: a shortcut for the p-value (* p < 0.1, ** p < 0.05, *** p < 0.01). More stars mean "harder to blame on chance", not "bigger effect".
- **R²** (bottom): the share of the variance of life expectancy accounted for by the model in that column.
- **Num.Obs.** (N): the number of countries used. It changes between columns (1) and (2): compare the sample before comparing the estimates.
:::

How to read the four columns together (the table above has all the numbers):

- Columns (1) and (2) give the same kind of estimate on two samples: the GDP coefficient is 5.65 with all countries and 5.27 with the countries that have a Gini (N = 198 vs 78). Sample composition matters.
- Column (3): adding the Gini barely moves the GDP coefficient (5.27 to 5.31). The Gini coefficient is 0.014 (robust SE 0.047, p = 0.76): a 10-point higher Gini goes with about 0.14 years of life expectancy, holding income constant. That is small, and statistically indistinguishable from zero.
- Column (4): with region dummies the GDP coefficient is 5.14 and the Gini coefficient 0.027 (p = 0.68), again small and not significant. The income gradient survives the controls; it is not simply a regional effect.


::: {.cell}

